{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions","id":1553978,"title":"A couple of celerity mechanics questions.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-09-23T07:47:56-07:00","id":1553978,"title":"A couple of celerity mechanics questions.","content":"Hey folks, my MtAw chronicle is coming to an end, and I will be doing a Requiem Chronicle next. <br /> So I was reviewing some rules, and about celerity specifically got me some questions that I would like to hear other people takes on it.<br /> <br /> - Let's say you are fighting 1v1, and you are last on initiative. You spend vitae to go first and take your turn. The NPC take two consecutive turns unless you spend vitae again?<br /> <br /> - If I decide to interrupt someone, what can I do? In my head it was just taking an action or just moving, not both. But if we look at the book, it just says that we can't take multiple instant actions, which doesn't say nothing about moving and acting neither clarifies if it's in the entire &quot;round&quot; that you can't take multiple actions, until it is your turn again or if it is just in the small action provided by interrupt. However the second option would potentially give the opportunity to a character act twice in a &quot;round&quot; which seems against CofD philosophy. I wouldn't specially mind it, since it's a little exception, kind costly and limited per scene, but I am not sure which is the intention here.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26883-imortalkiller","name":"ImortalKiller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-23T09:09:29-07:00","id":1553981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ImortalKiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions#post1553978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Let's say you are fighting 1v1, and you are last on initiative. You spend vitae to go first and take your turn. The NPC take two consecutive turns unless you spend vitae again?</div> </div> </div> </div>Correct, with the caveat that unless you waited until a third party already moved you would also have taken two consecutive turns.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - If I decide to interrupt someone, what can I do? In my head it was just taking an action or just moving, not both. But if we look at the book, it just says that we can't take multiple instant actions, which doesn't say nothing about moving and acting neither clarifies if it's in the entire &quot;round&quot; that you can't take multiple actions, until it is your turn again or if it is just in the small action provided by interrupt. However the second option would potentially give the opportunity to a character act twice in a &quot;round&quot; which seems against CofD philosophy. I wouldn't specially mind it, since it's a little exception, kind costly and limited per scene, but I am not sure which is the intention here. </div> </div> </div>For simplicity's sake, because of the lack of explicit statement, and because of the metered nature of its usage, I would say it lets you take an instant action or a movement; a brief action is not a full turn of possible activity.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-23T09:15:47-07:00","id":1553982,"title":"","content":"Yes, the NPC takes two consecutive turns. But most battles are greatly affected by the very first action, and at any moment being able to change initiatives is an advantage.<br /> <br /> If you interrupt someone you get your full action (including movement) right then and there. You don't act again in the round.<br /> <br /> Both are meant as opportunistic effects, they are extremely effective despite being seemingly weak because they play havoc on the course of a battle.<br /> <br /> On many, many occasions, the first action of a battle is the most important action of the battle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-23T09:44:17-07:00","id":1553983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions?p=1553981#post1553981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> For simplicity's sake, because of the lack of explicit statement, and because of the metered nature of its usage, I would say it lets you take an instant action or a movement; a brief action is not a full turn of possible activity.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's my reading too, in my mind I just remembered it being more straight forward saying that, or having a clarification of it somewhere.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions?p=1553982#post1553982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you interrupt someone you get your full action (including movement) right then and there. You don't act again in the round.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't believe that I can agree with that, specifically with the part that you don't act again. Sure this works if you are later on the initiative, however if you jump to the top, or just simply roll better, you either just don't have access to the interrupt anymore, or have two full turns in a round and losing your next turn (I am assuming you believe in the former). I don't believe that interrupting is just an on-demand duplicate of the jump forward on the initiative ability, even more because the system mention that several effects can be called simultaneously, but each one just once per turn, which would be really weird with that read, because they just don't make sense being used simultaneously if I got your interpretation right<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26883-imortalkiller","name":"ImortalKiller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-23T17:00:37-07:00","id":1553986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ImortalKiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions?p=1553983#post1553983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">even more because the system mention that several effects can be called simultaneously, but each one just once per turn, which would be really weird with that read, because they just don't make sense being used simultaneously if I got your interpretation right</div> </div> </div> </div>You're right. Anyway, Satchel's probably right.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-23T23:19:35-07:00","id":1553990,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ImortalKiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions#post1553978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> - If I decide to interrupt someone, what can I do? In my head it was just taking an action or just moving, not both. But if we look at the book, it just says that we can't take multiple instant actions, which doesn't say nothing about moving and acting neither clarifies if it's in the entire &quot;round&quot; that you can't take multiple actions, until it is your turn again or if it is just in the small action provided by interrupt. However the second option would potentially give the opportunity to a character act twice in a &quot;round&quot; which seems against CofD philosophy. I wouldn't specially mind it, since it's a little exception, kind costly and limited per scene, but I am not sure which is the intention here.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Interrupt is best used to avoid attacks for a hit and run. You can use movement OR an instant action. movement by RAW is not an instant action unless you sprint if I am remembering right). The best usage is if you are fighting someone in melee since you can move out of the way of an oncoming attack forcing them to miss and then on your turn you can attack them with a ranged attack assuming you still have your instant action.<br /> <br /> This is great for tearing down single opponents especially since they basically lose their action &quot;Once interrupted, the other character must continue his declared action, if it\u2019s still possible. If the action is no longer possible, he takes no action. Alternatively, that character\u2019s player may declare his action a dramatic failure and take a Beat.&quot;<br /> <br /> Notice it does not say another action it says NO action, celerity's interrupt is arguably one of the best abilities in the game which is why its usage is limited by your dots in the discipline, at mastery you basically have five turns of control over your opponent's actions if you use it right and the stars align.<br /> <br /> Rose Bailey had this to say back in 2019. In this thread <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1316215-celerity-and-movement\">Celerity and movement - Onyx Path Forums</a>\u200b<br /> <br /> <b>Short version: </b>Using Celerity to hit and run was intended. It gives you a chance to do something without other people being able to counter you, and that includes movement.<br /> <br /> <b>Longer version: </b>Back when we started the <i>Vampire</i> 2e project, I talked to the forums about cool stuff Celerity could do, and came away with the idea that it was more about the <i>effects</i> of speed rather than the ability to move a greater distance on your turn. Things like stopping a gunman before they pull the trigger, dodging a machete strike in the fraction of a second that the glint of the blade blinds the attacker, all your &quot;don't blink&quot; stuff.<br /> <br /> But we had to fit any kind of &quot;fast&quot; power into the turn/action system for combat. And we also had to keep Celerity meaningful for anyone who wanted to do a 1e foot-versus-vehicle chase or something, because neither GMC nor VtR2 were going to overhaul that system. We didn't want Celerity-dodging to require forfeiting your action, because we didn't want vampires on pure defense (lower case d). So it had to touch Initiative, Speed, and Defense.<br /> <br /> And that's all combat-ish -- at least three-quarters of what I've seen people do with Celerity in live play doesn't have anything to do with the combat system. Celerity needed to be narratively and mechanically clear when nobody was punching anybody.<br /> <br /> Rather than introduce new concepts into the combat system just to accommodate Celerity, we settled for a &quot;just works&quot; approach: the interrupt. We didn't want to allow multiple attacks (whole 'nother thing), so interrupts didn't give you a full instant action. Instead, we gave you guidelines on things you could do without anybody being able to stop you.<br /> <br /> As finished, it fits how a lot of people were using Celerity already (that forum thread, again), and makes it clear what it <i>does. </i>(The latter being the most important part of any Discipline.) But for it to work, you have to accept that interrupts are atomic -- they do what the rules say they do, and don't break down into instant or reflexive actions.<br /> <br /> You could, potentially, rewrite core combat so that &quot;fast&quot; isn't about initiative placement and discrete sprints. That's a pretty common playstyle already.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27607-bloodyrelight","name":"BloodyRelight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27607_2.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-25T11:32:58-07:00","id":1554012,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ImortalKiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1553978-a-couple-of-celerity-mechanics-questions?p=1553983#post1553983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's my reading too, in my mind I just remembered it being more straight forward saying that, or having a clarification of it somewhere.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I don't believe that I can agree with that, specifically with the part that you don't act again. Sure this works if you are later on the initiative, however if you jump to the top, or just simply roll better, you either just don't have access to the interrupt anymore, or have two full turns in a round and losing your next turn (I am assuming you believe in the former). I don't believe that interrupting is just an on-demand duplicate of the jump forward on the initiative ability, even more because the system mention that several effects can be called simultaneously, but each one just once per turn, which would be really weird with that read, because they just don't make sense being used simultaneously if I got your interpretation right<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>If you are first in initiative, acting in that turn, then interrupting opponent action, you basically just wasted a vitae. The interrupt allows you to use your action as an interruption, does not allow you to act twice.<br /> <br /> The difference with the jump in initiative one is that the opponent cannot change his declared action to react to yours. Which is a huge advantage since, e.g.: opponent delcare a grapple, you interrupt, punch him and use your movement as a perfect defense. Strong, but that's why it's limited to Celerity usage in a scene.<br /> <br /> With the normal initiative jump your opponent could actually chase you after your movement and still hit you.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Note you can always decide to postpone your action even if you were first in initiative","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 10-13-2025, 12:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}